          "question": "Do bodies differ about delayed prescribing for respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "The guidance I can access does not answer this question. The main source is the upper respiratory infection antibiotic stewardship guidance (an educational summary from an infectious diseases and public health stewardship body). It has a section on delayed prescribing, which is a prescription given with instructions to fill it only if symptoms do not improve or get worse by a set point. However, the text I received cuts off just as that section begins, so I cannot tell you what that section says or where authorities disagree.\n\nThe other documents I retrieved (on urinary tract infections, asthma, heart disease and diabetes) do not cover delayed prescribing for respiratory infections.\n\nTo find the answer, you could:\n- Ask a pharmacist or clinician, who can explain current views on delayed prescribing and why opinions differ.\n- Read the full upper respiratory infection stewardship guidance from its original publisher.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
